<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_create_elabel.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-createelabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-createelabel">
  <primary>CREATE ELABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE ELABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE ELABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>create an edge label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE [ UNLOGGED ] ELABEL [ IF NOT EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>
    [ ( <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> [, ...] ) ]
    [ DISABLE INDEX ]
    [ INHERITS ( <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable> [, ...] ) ]
    [ WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] ) ]
    [ TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable> ]

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ( <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> )
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ALWAYS AS ( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ) [ STORED ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createelabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   An edge label is a table with four columns: an <structfield>id</structfield> of type
   <type>graphid</type>, the <type>graphid</type> of the edge's <structfield>start</structfield>
   and <structfield>end</structfield> vertices, and a <structfield>properties</structfield>
   column of type <type>jsonb</type>. The label inherits the graph's
   <literal>ag_edge</literal> unless a parent is named. It is created in the graph named by
   <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot carry the graph.
  </para>

  <para>
   Creating one also creates the sequence that numbers its ids and three indexes: btree
   indexes on <literal>(start, "end")</literal> and <literal>("end", start)</literal>, which
   make a traversal fast in either direction, and a BRIN index on
   <structfield>id</structfield>. An edge label gets no primary key, because edges are reached
   through their endpoints. <structfield>start</structfield> and
   <structfield>end</structfield> are plain <type>graphid</type> columns, not foreign keys, so
   an edge label puts no restriction on which labels its endpoints have.
  </para>

  <para>
   The options are position sensitive: <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal>, then
   <literal>INHERITS</literal>, then <literal>WITH</literal>, then
   <literal>TABLESPACE</literal>. Any other order is a syntax error. Cypher also creates a
   label on demand, so <literal>CREATE (a)-[:directed]-&gt;(b)</literal> against a graph with
   no <literal>directed</literal> label creates one. Use <command>CREATE ELABEL</command> when
   the label needs options, promoted columns or a parent.
  </para>

  <para>
   A promoted property is a real typed column beside the property map, computed from the map
   and stored. Reads of that property are answered from the column instead of walking the JSON
   document, and the column can carry an index over the value's own type. This is worth doing
   for an edge weight a traversal filters or orders on. The three forms differ only in where
   the value comes from. <literal>rating numeric GENERATED</literal> mirrors the property whose
   key is the column name. <literal>source text GENERATED (site)</literal> mirrors the named
   key, so the column may be called something else. The long form computes the column from a
   SQL expression over the label's own columns.
  </para>

  <para>
   The property key comes from the generation expression, not from the column name, and is
   recorded in <structname>ag_label_property</structname>. A query therefore keeps naming the
   property, and the column name never appears in Cypher. Only a plain read of one key is
   recorded, such as <literal>properties -&gt;&gt; 'rating'</literal> with or without a cast.
   Wrap that read in anything else and the result is an ordinary generated column: SQL reads
   it, and <literal>e.<replaceable class="parameter">key</replaceable></literal> does not.
  </para>

  <para>
   Five things a promoted property cannot be. It cannot be left ungenerated, so a bare
   <literal><replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable
   class="parameter">data_type</replaceable></literal> is refused. It cannot be
   <literal>VIRTUAL</literal>, because a virtual generated column cannot carry an index;
   <literal>STORED</literal> is the default and may be left out. It cannot be
   <literal>GENERATED BY DEFAULT</literal>. On an edge label it cannot be named
   <structfield>id</structfield>, <structfield>properties</structfield>,
   <structfield>start</structfield> or <structfield>end</structfield>, the structural columns
   of a graph element. And it cannot carry a collation of its own: the property map compares
   strings with the database default, so a column collated otherwise would order and compare
   the same values differently, and which of the two answered a read would decide the result
   rather than the speed. That holds however the collation arrives &mdash; written down, brought
   by a domain, or given later by <link linkend="sql-altertable"><command>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER
   COLUMN ... TYPE</command></link>. A key an ancestor label already promotes is refused too,
   unless the column name is the same one.
  </para>

  <para>
   The data type has to have an immutable text input function and cannot fix a length.
   <type>date</type>, <type>timestamp</type>, <type>timestamptz</type> and array types are
   refused with <literal>generation expression is not immutable</literal>, and
   <type>varchar(n)</type> and <type>char(n)</type> because fitting a value to a length would
   let the column and the property hold different values. Accepted are <type>smallint</type>,
   <type>integer</type>, <type>bigint</type>, <type>numeric</type>, <type>real</type>,
   <type>double precision</type>, <type>text</type>, <type>boolean</type>, <type>uuid</type>
   and <type>jsonb</type>, and a type an extension provides such as <type>vector</type>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createelabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>UNLOGGED</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create the label's table as unlogged. Its contents are not written to the write ahead log
      and are lost after a crash.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error when a label of that name already exists. Nothing is
      created, and the existing label's options are left as they are.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the label to create, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A property to mirror into a typed column, in one of the three forms above. Columns are
      added after <structfield>properties</structfield>, in the order written.
      <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> is an identifier and is folded
      to lower case, matching the way Cypher stores map keys.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Create the label's three indexes but leave them invalid, so that loading rows does not
      maintain them. Reads and writes still work and the planner ignores an invalid index.
      Rebuild with <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/>. There is no <literal>ENABLE INDEX</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>INHERITS ( <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable> [, ...] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Parent labels, replacing the default parent <literal>ag_edge</literal>. Each has to be an
      edge label in the same graph; naming a vertex label is refused.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">storage_parameter</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ...] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Storage parameters for the label's table, as for <command>CREATE TABLE</command>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Tablespace for the label's table. Without it, <varname>default_tablespace</varname> decides.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createelabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They build
   a graph of their own and drop it at the end:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH elabeldemo;
SET graph_path = elabeldemo;
CREATE VLABEL person;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE ELABEL worked_on;
SELECT indexrelid::regclass AS index FROM pg_index
 WHERE indrelid = 'elabeldemo.worked_on'::regclass ORDER BY 1;

             index
--------------------------------
 elabeldemo.worked_on_id_idx
 elabeldemo.worked_on_start_idx
 elabeldemo.worked_on_end_idx
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Grouping several edge types under one parent lets a pattern match either kind, and
   <function>label()</function> still reports the label the edge was created with:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL acted_in INHERITS (worked_on);
CREATE ELABEL directed INHERITS (worked_on);
CREATE (:person {name: 'Denis Villeneuve'})-[:directed {credited: true}]-&gt;(:film {title: 'Arrival'});
MATCH (p)-[e:worked_on]-&gt;(f) RETURN label(e) AS type, e.credited AS credited;

    type    | credited
------------+----------
 "directed" | true
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Asking for a label that is there reports it and changes nothing:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL IF NOT EXISTS acted_in;

NOTICE:  label "acted_in" already exists, skipping
CREATE ELABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>CREATE UNLOGGED ELABEL staged DISABLE INDEX WITH (fillfactor = 70) TABLESPACE
   pg_default</literal> takes every option at once. All three indexes are then invalid, so a
   bulk load does not maintain them. Load the edges, then run
   <literal>REINDEX ELABEL staged</literal> to build the three.
  </para>

  <para>
   Promote two edge properties. <literal>rating</literal> takes its key from the column name
   and <literal>source</literal> is filled from the key <literal>site</literal>, so Cypher
   reads the second one as <literal>e.site</literal>:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL scored (rating numeric GENERATED, source text GENERATED (site));
MATCH (p:person), (f:film) CREATE (p)-[:scored {rating: 8.5, site: 'critics'}]-&gt;(f);
MATCH ()-[e:scored]-&gt;() RETURN e.rating AS rating, e.site AS site;

 rating |  site
--------+---------
    8.5 | critics
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   SQL reads the same two values under the column names, as
   <literal>SELECT rating, source FROM elabeldemo.scored</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge label can only inherit an edge label:
<programlisting>
CREATE ELABEL wrong INHERITS (person);

ERROR:  parent graph label "person" is not edge label.
</programlisting>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH elabeldemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createelabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   Creating an edge label also sets the statistics target of <structfield>start</structfield>
   and <structfield>end</structfield> to 1000, because a traversal's row estimate depends on
   how those two columns are distributed.
  </para>

  <para>
   <application>pg_dump</application> writes a label with an explicit label id, as
   <literal>CREATE ELABEL ONLY <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> (
   <replaceable class="parameter">label_id</replaceable> )</literal>, so that a restore keeps
   the ids a dumped graph handed out. That form creates no parent, no id default and no
   endpoint indexes. Write it only when reproducing a dump.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-createelabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no label DDL. An edge is described by an edge type inside a graph
   type, clause 12.6, which names the edge's endpoint node types and its properties and
   constrains a graph that references it. AgensGraph declares edge labels one at a time, and an
   edge label constrains neither its endpoints nor its properties.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE ELABEL</command> corresponds to <command>CREATE TABLE</command>.
   <literal>UNLOGGED</literal>, <literal>IF NOT EXISTS</literal>, <literal>INHERITS</literal>,
   <literal>WITH</literal> and <literal>TABLESPACE</literal> mean there what they mean here,
   and each promoted property becomes a <literal>GENERATED ALWAYS AS ... STORED</literal>
   column. The differences are the fixed four column shape, the three indexes created for you,
   the registration in <structname>ag_label</structname> and
   <structname>ag_label_property</structname>, the automatic parent, and a fixed option order.
   Because <structfield>start</structfield> and <structfield>end</structfield> are not foreign
   keys, nothing at the table level stops an edge from pointing at a vertex that has been
   deleted; <xref linkend="cypher-dropvlabel"/> handles that. Going the other way, a label's
   table cannot be reshaped with <command>ALTER TABLE</command> unless
   <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is on.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

